Revision history for Cn-Bioinf

0.01 2026-09-30 CDT

 [First release]

 - fasta2hash, hash2fasta_file, get_best_alignment_hit, msa_quality_table
   and clustal_view_residues, taken from the maintainer's bioinf.pm.
   fasta2hash and hash2fasta_file are now XS: on a 928 MB FASTA, reading
   went from 3.83 s to under 1 s and writing from 44.44 s to 0.97 s.

 - bioinf.pm's msa_phylo_plot is split in two. plot_msa aligns sequences
   with Clustal Omega and draws the alignment; plot_phylo draws the guide
   tree, either aligning the sequences itself or drawing a tree file that
   plot_msa kept, so the two images need only one alignment.

 - clustalo, BLAST+ and the plotting Python come from Alien::Bioinf, never
   from PATH. Alien::Bioinf is recommended rather than required, since it
   installs only where NCBI builds BLAST+; the functions that need it say so
   when it is missing, and t/msa.t is skipped without it.

 - fasta2hash drops whitespace from sequences and from the end of
   deflines, as Biopython does, and reads CRLF files; with a key, it stops
   at the record after that one. hash2fasta_file writes keys and
   sequences as bytes, and dies on a character above 255.

 - get_best_alignment_hit returns each query's hits as an array, best
   first, so two hits of one title are both kept, and gives every
   description of a hit in "descriptions", so a search of nr, which lists
   one sequence under several accessions, can be read.

 - Sequence names may hold spaces and punctuation. Clustal Omega is given
   the sequences numbered, and the alignment and the guide tree get their
   names back, quoted in the tree as newick quotes them.

 - Every image's provenance is one line of Creator metadata, and the only
   metadata written, as from Matplotlib::Simple: the script, the user, the
   host, the perl, Python and library versions, and the inputs, each with
   its SHA-256, with the clustalo or blastp command that made them.

 - pgs_score computes a polygenic score for each sample of a VCF from a
   PGS Catalog scoring file, matching variants by position or by rsID. The
   VCF may be a file (VCF, BCF or a PLINK 2 .pgen) or a data frame as
   Stats::LikeR's read_table returns it, and so may the scoring file.
   PLINK 2, which Alien::Bioinf now installs, does the scoring, so perl
   never holds the genotypes: 1.77 million variants against a whole genome
   took 25 s, perl peaking at 0.8 GB. It says how many variants were
   scored, missing, or found with alleles that disagree, and warns when the
   disagreement suggests two genome builds, or when a VCF lists only the
   sites where its sample carries an ALT allele.

 - sex_check tells each sample of a VCF (.vcf, .vcf.gz or .vcf.bz2) as
   male or female by peddy's method: its ratio of heterozygous to
   homozygous-ALT SNVs on X outside the pseudo-autosomal regions, male
   below 0.6. It prints a table of each sample's sex and the counts it
   was told from, and returns them as a hash. Perl decompresses the file
   and XS counts it, keeping seven counters a sample, so a whole genome's
   .vcf.gz took 4.8 s and 47 MB.
 - parent returns 1 when two people are parent and child and 0 when not,
   from two samples of one VCF or each person's own VCF: their IBS0 rate
   (opposite homozygotes) must be below peddy's 0.012, and their
   KING-robust kinship, which matches PLINK 2's, from 0.177 to 0.354. It
   prints the counts it decided from, and returns them too in list
   context. A site that one person's own VCF lacks is taken as hom-REF, so
   it warns when one file has far more sites alone than the other, as an
   exome against a genome does. Two exomes took 1.6 s.
 - relatedness tells how every two people are related, from every sample
   of one VCF or the one sample of each of several: each pair's
   KING-robust kinship and IBS0, IBS1 and IBS2 counts, as parent takes
   them, a relationship (parent-child, full siblings, duplicate, second-
   or third-degree, unrelated), and a category named from the sexes it
   tells from X in the same reading (father-son, mother-daughter,
   brother-sister, brothers, sisters). Which of a parent and child is the
   parent is said where a third person shows it. It returns a hash of
   hashes, $r->{a}{b} being the same hash as $r->{b}{a}, and a pair that
   parent would die on is left undecided, with the reason, rather than
   stopping the rest. XS counts every pair 64 sites at a time: the 17
   people of the CEPH 1463 pedigree took 0.27 s, and agreed with it on
   every sex and on 134 of 136 pairs, and three exomes took 2.5 s.
 - concordance compares a test VCF's calls with a truth VCF's, sample by
   sample, or the one sample of each whatever they are called, and counts
   the 45 kinds of agreement and disagreement and the metrics of the
   maintainer's x.low_ram_local_concordance.pl, with each discordant,
   gained and lost site. Unlike that script it finds a site whatever its
   ID and with "chr" in both files, compares calls by their alleles, so 1/0
   and 0|1 are 0/1, and takes ./1 as a no-call, where it died. Its true
   positives are corrected to the matched calls that carry an ALT, where
   the script counted matched hom-REF calls and missed matched het-REF
   ones, and its true negatives to every matched reference call, haploid
   or diploid; a discordance of two ALT-carrying calls (0/1 against 1/1)
   is a false positive and a false negative, where the script counted it
   as neither. XS compares them, holding only the test's sites: two exomes
   took 1.9 s, and a genome against itself 14 s.
 - concordance dies, before reading any records, when the two VCFs'
   ##contig lines give one contig two lengths, as GRCh37 and GRCh38 do,
   and names the contigs and each header's build. Two such files used to
   be compared position by position, which matched few sites, and those
   by chance.
 - hirisplex_genotypes reads the 41 markers of the HIrisPlex-S eye, hair
   and skin colour panel out of a VCF and counts, for each sample, how
   many copies of each marker's model allele it carries, writing them as
   the CSV the HIrisPlex-S webtool uploads. The panel ships as
   share/hirisplex_s_panel.tsv, written by share/make_hirisplex_panel.pl
   from the webtool's own input form and Ensembl; no prediction model
   ships, because HIrisPlex-S's coefficients are not published and part of
   the knowledge behind them is covered by European patent EP2195448.
   Markers are matched by position on GRCh37 or GRCh38, read from the
   VCF's ##contig lengths unless "build" says, or by rs number. The panel
   records the forward-strand allele to count, so nothing is guessed: 14
   of the 41 are the complement of the allele the webtool names, ASIP
   rs6119471 carries the named allele on the forward strand and still has
   to be flipped, as the HIrisPlex-S manual says, and MC1R rs312262906 is
   the 86insA frameshift, merged into rs796296176, which is counted as the
   allele longer than REF. XS reads the file in one pass, keeping one byte
   per sample per marker. Each sample is returned with which predictions
   the manual's rules allow at all: no eye colour without HERC2
   rs12913832, no hair colour when all 11 MC1R markers are missing.
 - trait_predict applies a multinomial logistic regression, which is what
   the IrisPlex, HIrisPlex and HIrisPlex-S models are, to those genotypes
   and a model table the caller supplies, and returns a probability for
   each category of each trait. A marker with no genotype drops out of the
   sum, which counts it as 0 copies, and the markers used and missing are
   returned with every prediction so that one made from few markers can be
   reported as such. A trait that hirisplex_genotypes says cannot be
   predicted is not, unless "force" is 1.
 - pdb_search searches the RCSB PDB's structures for a sequence, and
   returns the matching polymer entities best first, each with how well it
   aligns (E-value, bit score, identity, coverage, gaps and the aligned
   sequences, under get_best_alignment_hit's names) and what kind of
   structure it is: method, resolution or a computed model's pLDDT,
   oligomeric state and symmetry, chains, organism, UniProt, Pfam and
   InterPro entries, and ligands. It prints a table of them. RCSB aligns
   with MMseqs2, so the scores are MMseqs2's, not blastp's. It needs the
   network and HTTPS, so IO::Socket::SSL and Net::SSLeay are recommended,
   and HTTP::Tiny 0.056 is now required.
 - plot_msa and plot_phylo take "fh" and "execute", as Matplotlib::Simple's
   plt does. Each image is drawn by a Python script that stands alone,
   appended to "fh", a File::Temp, when one is given; "execute => 0"
   writes the script without running it, so several images, theirs and
   Matplotlib::Simple's, can be drawn by one run of Python. The script
   carries the alignment or tree it draws, so it still runs after perl's
   temporary files are gone. Both functions now return "python_file", the
   script's name, and print "will write" rather than "wrote" for an image
   not drawn yet.
 - plot_msa takes "tooltips => 1", which gives each residue of an SVG
   alignment a tooltip that a browser shows on hovering over it, naming
   the sequence, the residue and its number, and the alignment column, as
   "S.cerevisiae: Lys100, alignment column 105".
 - hash2fasta_file prints "wrote" and the file's name to STDOUT, as
   plot_msa and clustal_view_residues do, where it used to say nothing. The
   FASTA files that plot_msa and plot_phylo write for themselves are not
   announced.
 - sex_check, parent, relatedness, concordance and hirisplex_genotypes
   could lose their results, or corrupt the heap and die with
   "realloc(): invalid next size", when perl's stack had to grow while the
   VCF was being read, by the perl that reads and decompresses it. Their
   XS now takes the stack back from perl after reading, where it used to
   push the results through a pointer into the stack as it was before.
   Found by review on 2026-10-26.
 - The tests failed when TMPDIR held a space, as a Windows user name can:
   35 subtests of t/sex.t, t/parent.t, t/relatedness.t and t/msa.t matched
   a temporary file's name with \S+. They now pass with a space in it.
   Found by review on 2026-11-02.
 - pgs_score died with PLINK 2's "All N variant(s) in --vcf file excluded
   by --chr" (exit 7) when the VCF had none of the score's chromosomes. It
   now scores every variant missing, as it does when only the positions are
   absent. When PLINK 2 does not start at all, the message gives the reason
   system gave, where it gave "Inappropriate ioctl for device". Found by
   review on 2026-11-02.
 - hirisplex_genotypes counted any ALT longer than REF as MC1R's 86insA
   (rs312262906), so an MNV such as AC to GTT, or a symbolic <INS>, at that
   position was counted as two copies of it. It now counts only an ALT that
   is REF with one A put in, however the VCF anchors the indel. And bases
   are compared regardless of case, where a lower-case REF or ALT matched
   nothing and left the marker 0 copies. Found by review on 2026-11-02.
 - trait_predict multiplied in whatever a dosage held, so "NA" was a called
   genotype of 0 copies and 7 was taken as it was. A dosage of a marker the
   model uses must now be a number from 0 to 2, fractional ones included,
   or undef for no genotype; anything else dies naming the sample, the
   marker and the value. Found by review on 2026-11-02.
 - plot_msa and plot_phylo died, once clustalo had run, when a title or
   label began with "-" (such as "-log10"), or a file name did: argparse
   read it as an option. Every option now reaches msa_plot.py joined to its
   value, as --t=-log10. Found by review on 2026-11-02.
 - sex_check read a GT cut short, such as "1/", as the haploid call "1",
   and counted it as haploid hom-ALT on X. It is now missing. Found by
   review on 2026-11-02.
 - get_best_alignment_hit and msa_quality_table died with JSON::MaybeXS's
   message or with perl's "Not a HASH reference", naming no function, for a
   file that was not JSON or not a BLAST report; get_best_alignment_hit
   returned no queries for {}, and named a query that had no title "" with
   an "uninitialized" warning. Each now dies naming the function and what
   is wrong, and a query with no title is named by its query_id. Found by
   review on 2026-11-02.
 - pdb_search died of "Illegal division by zero" for a hit RCSB gave an
   alignment or query length of 0, and with perl's own messages for an
   answer that was JSON but not a search result. Such a hit's pident or
   query_cover is now undef, and shown as "-" in the report; such an answer
   dies naming pdb_search. Found by review on 2026-11-02.
 - plot_msa, plot_phylo and msa_quality_table passed a sequence that was
   undef, a reference or empty on to clustalo or blastp, which failed with
   messages that named none of them. Each is now refused first, naming the
   function and the sequence. Found by review on 2026-11-02.
 - fasta2hash read a file ending in .GZ, or any case but .gz, as plain
   text. Its warning of a repeated defline printed the whitespace dropped
   from the defline's end, the CR of a CRLF file included. Found by review
   on 2026-11-02.
 - clustal_view_residues left |, < and > in a name as they were, which
   LaTeX's default fonts print as an em dash and an inverted ! and ?, and
   did not escape the residues at all. A residue number in the last columns
   of a block was left off the track row, with every number after it in
   that block; it now ends at the block's end. A tracked protein that
   "order" leaves out was counted as having a track row, so a table held
   fewer blocks than "split" said. Found by review on 2026-11-02.
